<Layout
  title={"Writing a Game Once and Running It Everywhere: Where Exact Replay Ends and Tolerance Begins"}
  description={"About: Writing a Game Once and Running It Everywhere. Executed 3-OS × 3-ISA GE-H1 (0/25,500 SHA-256 mismatches across 30 cells on arm64 macOS, aarch64 Linux, AMD/Intel x86_64 Linux, and Intel x86_64 Windows 11), 3-OS GE-H2 (t_div = 10–37 across Windows UCRT, Apple libm, and GNU glibc sin/cos; t_div = 192–406 MSVC vs Clang and x86_64 vs arm64 FMA), and 3-API GE-H3 wgpu DirectX 12/Metal/Vulkan tolerance (28/28 hardware GPU×API pairs pass gate: PSNR >= 64.16 dB, SSIM >= 0.99983, max pixel diff <= 2/255)."}
>
  <section class="px-6 md:px-10 pt-16 md:pt-24 pb-20 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="section-label mb-4">Research study · about</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-brand-white tracking-tight">
      Writing a Game Once and Running It Everywhere: Where Exact Replay Ends and Tolerance Begins
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 font-body text-base text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
      This study asks whether a game can be written once as a checked state machine and then projected across languages, operating systems, CPU instruction set architectures, and GPU rendering stacks. Both the surveyed specifications and three executed falsifier suites (Section 6) establish the boundary: <strong>yes</strong> for game rules and simulation under pinned integer arithmetic (<code>GE-H1</code>: <code>0 / 25,500</code> <code>SHA-256</code> checkpoint mismatches across <code>30</code> target × host cells spanning <code>arm64</code> macOS, <code>aarch64</code> Linux, AMD/Intel <code>x86_64</code> Linux, and bare-metal Intel <code>x86_64</code> Windows 11); <strong>no</strong> when standard library transcendentals or fused multiply-add lowering cross OS/compiler/ISA boundaries in chaotic collisions (<code>GE-H2</code> across Windows <code>UCRT</code>, Apple <code>libSystem</code>, and GNU <code>glibc</code>); and <strong>no</strong> for bit-exact pixels across graphics APIs and vendors, yet <strong>yes</strong> under a checked perceptual tolerance gate (<code>GE-H3</code> across <code>8</code> hardware <code>GPU × API</code> endpoints spanning Windows <code>DirectX 12</code>, Apple <code>Metal</code>, and Linux/Windows <code>Vulkan</code>; <code>measured_j = None</code>, <code>board_metered = false</code>).
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 glass-card p-6">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[2px] uppercase text-brand-gray mb-3">In plain words</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
        <li>Under <code>Q32.32</code> 64-bit integer physics (<code>GE-H1</code>, <code>100,000</code> ticks), a 16-body 2D arena replays bit-for-bit across native Rust, C++20 (Clang, GCC, and Windows MSVC <code>cl.exe</code>), WebAssembly, Node.js <code>BigInt</code>, and Python across six machines spanning macOS, Linux, and Windows 11 (<code>0 / 25,500</code> <code>SHA-256</code> mismatches; <code>0.073–13.60 J</code> macOS <code>L3</code> <code>reported_j</code>; <code>1.89–181.87 J</code> Linux <code>L2</code> RAPL package <code>reported_j</code>; <code>138.9M–24,722.2M</code> Win32 <code>L3</code> CPU cycles).</li>
        <li>Under IEEE 754 floating-point physics (<code>GE-H2</code>, <code>25,000</code> ticks), even <em>without</em> <code>-ffast-math</code> or <code>/fp:fast</code>, all three desktop OS math libraries form a pairwise-divergent triangle on <code>std::sin</code>/<code>std::cos</code> — Windows <code>UCRT</code> vs Linux <code>glibc</code> diverges on <code>x86_64</code> at tick <code>10</code> (gameplay desync at tick <code>10,392</code>), Apple <code>libSystem</code> vs <code>glibc</code> at tick <code>10</code> (<code>9,798</code>), and Windows <code>UCRT</code> vs Apple <code>libSystem</code> at tick <code>37</code> (<code>9,798</code>) — while default <code>MSVC /fp:precise</code> vs <code>Clang -ffp-contract=fast</code> diverges at tick <code>192</code> and <code>x86_64</code> <code>VFMADD</code> vs <code>arm64</code> <code>FMADD</code> diverges at tick <code>406</code>.</li>
        <li>Across eight hardware <code>GPU × API</code> endpoints on headless <code>wgpu</code> (<code>GE-H3</code>, <code>512×512</code> <code>RGBA8Unorm</code> across <code>DirectX 12</code>, <code>Metal</code>, and <code>Vulkan</code> plus <code>WARP</code>, <code>llvmpipe</code>, and <code>CPU Q16.16</code>), even switching <code>DirectX 12</code> (<code>HLSL</code>/<code>DXIL</code>) vs <code>Vulkan</code> (<code>SPIR-V</code>) on the <em>same</em> Windows 11 GPU alters <code>1</code> LSB via shader lowering (<code>108.34 dB</code> PSNR on <code>RTX 2060</code>, <code>79.52 dB</code> on <code>Intel Iris Xe</code>), yet all <code>28 / 28</code> hardware endpoint pairs pass perceptual tolerance (<code>PSNR ≥ 64.16 dB</code>, <code>SSIM ≥ 0.99983</code>, <code>Δ_max ≤ 2</code>). Explore the 3-OS / 3-API inspector and playable joule-budgeted <code>Q32.32</code> Chrono-Budget Arena at <a href="/living/game-engines/" class="text-brand-cyan underline">/living/game-engines/</a>.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

